{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"b2ca5d6a866045b8ee4e827f5b3393725742b354c7faa36fe3c6aa442fb678b2","slug":"interpolation","location":"/interpolation.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Interpolation and Density","content_includes_title":false,"github":"https://github.com/whitschroder/gis","keywords":["GIS","archaeology"],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"thumbnail":"/interpolation-060ef84c686b37cd2a5f3a44b88aa4af.jpg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"interpolation.md","url":"/interpolation-9ec984ddd199a0c3f7ffa579e5d782d2.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Often discussed together, interpolation and density tools are actually distinct approaches to generating a continuous surface from sample data points.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"rpGqH4tuXn"}],"key":"RFk7hgz8Fy"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"Dg71nJipDU"}],"key":"gwI8bYxpEL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"QGIS inaccurately lumps interpolation and density tools together under ","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"WNJ6qoqZac"},{"type":"link","url":"https://docs.qgis.org/3.44/en/docs/user_manual/processing_algs/qgis/interpolation.html","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interpolation","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"V5QqUSx8q0"}],"urlSource":"https://docs.qgis.org/3.44/en/docs/user_manual/processing_algs/qgis/interpolation.html","key":"faMtuyB6YN"},{"type":"text","value":", while ArcGIS Pro treats the two separately in the Geoprocessing toolbox.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"JcPADn13yp"}],"key":"Oy2stvKBfM"}],"key":"vPmzsEk9NV"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Density","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"mZQLlTZrD2"}],"identifier":"density","label":"Density","html_id":"density","implicit":true,"key":"M1f6NTM1KH"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The density tools should only be applied to discrete point data that represents a specific occurrence of some phenomenon; for example, artifacts documented on an archaeological surface survey. In such a case, each point represents a frequency of the occurrence of data, or number of artifacts. In this scenario, the presence of an artifact in one location does not necessarily influence the presence of an artifact nearby. The density tools will therefore not make up locations of samples but rather generate a surface that shows “hot spots” of higher vs. lower frequencies.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"DNX4SdSgYN"}],"key":"Hy1eYzF2bv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Density can be visualized in several ways. The ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"RlmIY1pP7J"},{"type":"link","url":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/point-density.htm","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Point Density tool","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"WICn0YKBrl"}],"urlSource":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/point-density.htm","key":"gvzUMiSxTN"},{"type":"text","value":" in ArcGIS Pro will calculate the number of samples within a specified radius or neighborhood (typically a circle). If each point represents a single occurrence of a phenomenon (e.g., a single artifact), the Population field can be blank. However, if each point contains a field representing a count of the phenomenon (e.g., multiple artifacts at one point), the Population field can be updated to include this value. The output will be several circles (or other shapes dependent on the type of Neighborhood selected) with colors assigned to those with higher counts/densities of point data. Three dimensionally, this surface would look like several overlapping cylinders whose heights reflect the point density.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"rPNhg5suDj"}],"key":"zAT3dMEj5t"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"xCZTB6x0Uh"}],"key":"zOqKASnxtu"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A similar tool in QGIS is Grid (Data metrics) under GDAL. For density, the Data metric to use should be set to Count. The first radius of search ellipse and second radius of search ellipse can be set to the same value as they refer to the width and height of the search radius. If each point contains an additional field representing a count of the phenomenon or other value, the Z value from field under Advanced Parameters can be set to the appropriate field.","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"PHSsfzlPB0"}],"key":"mFuM6O0fw1"}],"key":"B3eZmMb16L"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"nKJzOJYC4C"},{"type":"link","url":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/kernel-density.htm","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kernel Density tool","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"y1jStf78dy"}],"urlSource":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/kernel-density.htm","key":"xT4eYPRPYc"},{"type":"text","value":" in ArcGIS Pro is similar to point density; however, the defined radius is known as a kernel, bandwidth, or bin that determines how density will be visualized. In ArcGIS Pro, this kernel is defined with the Search radius parameter. This parameter can significantly affect the results of the density analysis. If left blank, a default value will be calculated using Silverman’s Rule of Thumb. Based on the distribution of the data, this approach is not always the appropriate one. Best practice is to run the kernel density analysis with several different parameters. The output from the kernel density tool is smoother than that of the point density tool, and if visualized three dimensionally would represent mounded features rather than cylinders.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"GC0k5agdh9"}],"key":"JeVq69vDLW"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"uz7vOe4ZEB"}],"key":"ZJx8w648wo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In QGIS, the Heatmap (Kernel Density Estimation) tool is available under ","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"EOjytvL24C"},{"type":"link","url":"https://docs.qgis.org/3.44/en/docs/user_manual/processing_algs/qgis/interpolation.html","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interpolation","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"geIqgWTUCr"}],"urlSource":"https://docs.qgis.org/3.44/en/docs/user_manual/processing_algs/qgis/interpolation.html","key":"VXj8GFxu2G"},{"type":"text","value":".","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"cwfRJi3Udd"}],"key":"IaY01DgNkM"}],"key":"QqFPXd6w3c"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interpolation","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"eK8UY2sSEB"}],"identifier":"interpolation","label":"Interpolation","html_id":"interpolation","implicit":true,"key":"FRtg2Pm0Ig"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The interpolation tools, in contrast to the density tools, should be used on continuous data, where values are sampled at specified points. Interpolation assumes that values will be most similar to the closest values and more different from the farthest values. In other words, in contrast to the density tools, the interpolation tools assume that the value of sample data in one place will influence the values of nearby points. The interpolation tools also differ from the density tools in that a Z value field is required, which in terrain analysis represents the elevation of point data. Several interpolation approaches are available in ArcGIS Pro under the Interpolation tools in the Geoprocessing toolbox.","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"nV1Qos2Edh"}],"key":"cBf28sKK7i"},{"type":"image","url":"/interpolation-060ef84c686b37cd2a5f3a44b88aa4af.jpg","alt":"Interpolation","width":"50%","align":"center","class":"bg-primary mb-1","key":"FrauzR51Gp","urlSource":"/images/interpolation.jpg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"ktK3A5pQqI"},{"type":"link","url":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/idw.htm","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"IDW tool","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"jHcaAQ9BGZ"}],"urlSource":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/idw.htm","key":"Ij11lITw3z"},{"type":"text","value":" in ArcGIS Pro conducts an interpolation based on Inverse Distance Weighting. The Power parameter will determine how strongly nearby points affect the interpolated value. Higher powers will assign less influence from more distance points.","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"tKCdNaqqtg"}],"key":"EAX0LYoB2t"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"BQ79EMf62e"}],"key":"HsBGN4IyT4"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This method is also available in QGIS using the IDW interpolation tool under Interpolation.","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"vkyQIBnJr8"}],"key":"VY08fCnsks"}],"key":"AiULT1bhro"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"RLNkbpcB5v"},{"type":"link","url":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.3/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/kriging.htm","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kriging tool","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"hgIBdjw86Y"}],"urlSource":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.3/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/kriging.htm","key":"QxqbnDwscL"},{"type":"text","value":" provides a geostatistical technique to interpolation. This tool will also optionally output a variance of prediction raster that includes error values. The ","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"S9HZ6zXB8a"},{"type":"link","url":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/geostatistical-analyst/empirical-bayesian-kriging.htm","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Empirical Bayesian Kriging tool","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"ZhdlvKXosV"}],"urlSource":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/geostatistical-analyst/empirical-bayesian-kriging.htm","key":"PXqDcu7tUo"},{"type":"text","value":" is a more advanced application of this technique.","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"pFmHDwV7No"}],"key":"Iy0ukQGJAX"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"N2tearAXQ0"}],"key":"go3zr2dyd1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In QGIS, several options are available under the Whitebox Workflows plugin, including Ordinary Kriging Interpolation and Ordinary CoKriging Interpolation.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"Z8FFY9q97q"}],"key":"jc64UvHHOL"}],"key":"fBJ7RVxUcC"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"uq2nTE3p4s"},{"type":"link","url":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/natural-neighbor.htm","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Natural Neighbor tool","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"XWWcHyL3gc"}],"urlSource":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/natural-neighbor.htm","key":"oh9KW97OKi"},{"type":"text","value":" is the simplest approach to interpolation. This tool should generally be reserved only for modelling flat areas with minimal topographic variation.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"QGQuaOc8vN"}],"key":"TJhf6R5rZV"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"fxgTZ4xeVX"}],"key":"FfO43sw0Lq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In QGIS, Natural Neighbour Interpolation is available under the Whitebox Workflows plugin.","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"vlFlClP5oP"}],"key":"M3QOdRaLEM"}],"key":"oxNHFQN5sY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"FpuUZskJ6n"},{"type":"link","url":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.3/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/spline.htm","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Spline tool","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"E75Pk99HLg"}],"urlSource":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.3/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/spline.htm","key":"phCJHkqSuY"},{"type":"text","value":" uses a two-dimensional minimum curvature spline technique that constrains the resulting surface to pass through the sample points. The ","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"zWodwC6jnN"},{"type":"link","url":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/latest/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/spline-with-barriers.htm","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Spline with Barriers tool","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"stGCrqxAGe"}],"urlSource":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/latest/tool-reference/spatial-analyst/spline-with-barriers.htm","key":"DYcxp8qgTx"},{"type":"text","value":" conducts a spline interpolation within a barrier feature class (polyline or polygon) to remove edge effects.","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"OzVHr3jhar"}],"key":"Gg2j9m3DeB"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"klbJ9p93Wl"}],"key":"Y0hv2uXlQy"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In QGIS, Multilevel B-Spline and Thin Plate Spline are available under SAGA Next Gen.","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"Iz0jiC0BHJ"}],"key":"xM1PVhQ3L1"}],"key":"rwaSx2tTf5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that edge effects can be removed from any of these approaches by using the ","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"key":"GlvsmYlG6y"},{"type":"link","url":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/data-management/clip.htm","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clip Raster tool","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"key":"mamwhTWmEe"}],"urlSource":"https://pro.arcgis.com/en/pro-app/3.4/tool-reference/data-management/clip.htm","key":"eC5o43RGER"},{"type":"text","value":" on the output raster with an input polygon.","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"key":"ff4A87WX1F"}],"key":"mUEH665foD"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"VzJGSrqeb2"}],"key":"TpSvIKU6ZB"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In QGIS, use Clip raster by mask layer under GDAL or Clip Raster with Polygon under SAGA.","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"dXYfjrwFVh"}],"key":"nzvGPFYi8U"}],"key":"Xw2mAQfAxW"}],"key":"Z4YeTg80Iq"}],"key":"GWxv6nhysr"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}}}